    itemPurpose: 'independent-blocked',
    validationStatus: 'validated',
    stimulus:
      'Critics say the Gazette\u2019s use of anonymous sources makes its reporting untrustworthy. But there is a difference between granting anonymity to a whistleblower who fears retaliation and granting it to an official spinning a story. The Gazette\u2019s recent investigation relied on the first kind, not the second. So the criticism misses its mark.',
    stem: 'The argument proceeds by',
    choices: [
      { text: 'questioning the motives of the critics rather than addressing the substance of their argument against anonymity.' },
      { text: 'drawing a distinction between two kinds of anonymous sourcing and arguing that an objection to one does not apply to the other.' },
      { text: 'disputing the definition of trustworthy reporting without offering a clearer definition in its place.' },
      { text: 'presenting survey data on how readers view anonymous sources, then generalizing from that limited sample.' },
      { text: 'arguing that anonymous sourcing is common in journalism and therefore an acceptable practice.' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 1,
    explanationQuick:
      'The argument\u2019s pivot is the contrast between two kinds of anonymity — whistleblower protection versus official spin — followed by classifying the Gazette\u2019s case under the innocent kind. Splitting a criticized category and exempting the case at hand is the method of distinction.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Watch where the argument turns. It opens with the critics\u2019 blanket objection to anonymous sources, then pivots on "but there is a difference between" — the classic signal of a distinction being drawn. The distinction separates anonymity that shields vulnerable whistleblowers from anonymity that serves self-interested spin. The next sentence then classifies the Gazette\u2019s investigation under the first, innocent heading. The conclusion follows: a criticism aimed (at best) at the second kind misses a case of the first kind. That is the method of distinction: show the objection trades on lumping together things that differ in the relevant respect, then place the case at hand on the safe side of the split. The argument never impugns the critics\u2019 motives, never debates what "trustworthy" means, cites no surveys, and never appeals to how common the practice is.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect (trap: wrong-viewpoint). An ad hominem attack would target the critics\u2019 character or motives; this argument never mentions who the critics are or why they object. It engages the objection\u2019s content by narrowing its scope, which is the opposite of dodging it.',
      'Correct. The "difference between" sentence draws the distinction, the next sentence classifies the Gazette\u2019s investigation under the whistleblower heading, and the conclusion follows: the blanket criticism does not reach this case. Each step of the reasoning runs through the distinction.',
      'Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). The argument never contests what counts as trustworthy reporting; it accepts the critics\u2019 standard and argues the Gazette meets it in this instance. Redefining terms is a different method entirely.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). No surveys, polls, or reader data appear anywhere in the stimulus. A method choice must describe the argument\u2019s actual moves, and there is no empirical move here at all.',
      'Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The argument never claims the practice is widespread or that commonness would excuse it. Even if that were true, it would be a different argument from the distinction-based defense actually given.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      '"There is a difference between X and Y" is the signature of the method of distinction — the argument then typically classifies the case at hand on the favorable side of the split to blunt a general objection.',
