    itemPurpose: 'independent-blocked',
    validationStatus: 'validated',
    stimulus:
      'Mara: The community garden should switch to organic methods. The current pesticides are washing into the creek, and several gardeners have complained of skin irritation after spraying days.\n\nDevon: Switching to organic methods would cut our harvest nearly in half, and the garden\u2019s main purpose is to supply the food bank. The runoff problem can be handled with better drainage, and the irritation complaints are unproven.',
    stem: 'The point at issue between Mara and Devon is whether',
    choices: [
      { text: 'the garden should adopt organic growing methods.' },
      { text: 'the garden\u2019s harvest would decline under organic methods.' },
      { text: 'better drainage could reduce the pesticide runoff.' },
      { text: 'the food bank depends on the garden\u2019s harvest.' },
      { text: 'organic methods are more expensive than using pesticides.' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 0,
    explanationQuick:
      'Mara explicitly says the garden should switch to organic methods; Devon argues against switching (lost harvest, food-bank mission, runoff fixable another way). They directly contradict each other on what the garden should do.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'A point-at-issue answer must be a proposition one speaker affirms and the other denies. Start with each speaker\u2019s bottom line. Mara\u2019s first sentence is a "should" claim: the garden should switch to organic methods. Devon\u2019s response is a case against switching: it would halve the harvest, threaten the food-bank mission, and the runoff problem has another fix. Those positions are direct opposites on the same question — should the garden go organic? The distractors fail the affirm/deny test. The harvest decline is asserted only by Devon; Mara never addresses it. Better drainage is proposed only by Devon. Food-bank dependence and cost comparisons are addressed by neither speaker. Only the adoption question draws a "yes" from one speaker and a "no" from the other, which is exactly what "point at issue" requires.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Correct. Mara affirms it outright ("should switch to organic methods"), and Devon\u2019s entire reply is a case against switching — lost harvest, endangered food-bank mission, and an alternative fix for runoff. One says yes, the other says no: that is the definition of a point at issue.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Devon addresses the harvest effect; Mara says nothing about yields. A point at issue requires both speakers to take stands, and here only one does.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Better drainage is Devon\u2019s proposal alone; Mara never discusses drainage. With only one speaker on the record, there is no disagreement to identify.',
      'Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). Devon says supplying the food bank is the garden\u2019s main purpose, but neither speaker claims the food bank depends on the garden. The proposition as worded goes beyond anything either asserts.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Cost is never mentioned by either speaker. It may sound like a natural thing to dispute, but a point-at-issue answer must come from what the speakers actually said, not from what they might have said.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'For point-at-issue, test every candidate with the affirm/deny rule: one speaker must clearly say yes and the other clearly say no. If only one speaker addresses it, it cannot be the point at issue.',
    misconceptionTags: ['scope-shift'],
    trapTypes: ['unsupported', 'unsupported', 'answers-different-question', 'unsupported'],
    prerequisites: ['f-premise-conclusion'],
    estimatedSeconds: 60,
    sourceType: 'original',
    hints: [
      'What is Mara\u2019s bottom line — what does she think should happen? And what is Devon arguing for?',
      'For each answer choice, ask: does Mara take a stand on it? Does Devon? You need a yes from one and a no from the other.',
      'Which choice has Mara saying "should" and Devon effectively saying "should not"?',
    ],
    labels: { topic: 'community garden organic switch' },
    reviewHistory: [
      { date: '2026-09-11', reviewer: 'full-lr-classification-2026-09-11', verdict: 'validate',
        notes: 'KEEP; single defensible answer confirmed. Clean yes/no split on the should-adopt proposition; every distractor is addressed by only one speaker or neither. Difficulty d2 honest.' },
    ],
