    explanationQuick:
      'Ito predicts the program will lower street temperatures, while Okafor flatly denies it will deliver the promised cooling. That is a direct yes/no split on the program\u2019s effectiveness. The remaining options are conceded, one-sided, or broader than what either speaker claims, so they cannot be the point at issue.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Locate each speaker\u2019s verdict on the program\u2019s real-world effect. Ito\u2019s prediction is explicit: the program "will lower summer street temperatures by several degrees." Okafor\u2019s verdict is equally explicit and opposite: the program "will not deliver the promised cooling." Those two claims cannot both be true, so the program\u2019s effectiveness is genuinely at issue. The distractors dissolve under the affirm/deny test. Okafor says lab tests "don\u2019t settle the question," which is narrower than calling them generally unreliable — and Ito never defends their general reliability. The 40-percent reflectance figure is Ito\u2019s claim alone; Okafor never disputes the lab number, only its real-world relevance. The degradation and traffic claims appear only in Okafor\u2019s remarks. Only the program\u2019s cooling effect draws a yes from one speaker and a no from the other.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect (trap: too-strong). Okafor says lab tests "don\u2019t settle the question" — a narrow claim about this inference — not that lab tests are generally unreliable. This choice inflates her position beyond what she said, so it is not something she affirms.',
      'Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). The 40-percent figure is Ito\u2019s uncontested premise; Okafor never disputes the lab result, only whether it settles the real-world question. An undisputed premise cannot be the point at issue.',
      'Correct. Ito affirms the program will lower street temperatures by several degrees; Okafor denies it will deliver the promised cooling at all. One predicts success, the other predicts failure — a textbook point at issue.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Okafor asserts degradation under heavy traffic; Ito never addresses durability. A one-sided claim has no opponent and therefore cannot be what they disagree about.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Okafor compares the city\u2019s traffic to the test roads\u2019. Ito is silent on traffic levels, so there is no clash on this proposition.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'When one speaker cites lab results and the other disputes their real-world relevance, the point at issue is usually the real-world prediction itself — not the lab numbers, which often go uncontested.',
    misconceptionTags: ['scope-shift'],
    trapTypes: ['too-strong', 'supported-nonresponsive', 'unsupported', 'unsupported'],
