    itemPurpose: 'mixed-discrimination',
    validationStatus: 'validated',
    stimulus:
      'Alvarez: Congestion pricing has cut traffic in every city that has tried it, and our downtown gridlock is worse than any of theirs was. If we want cleaner air and faster buses, we should adopt it here.\n\nBrooks: Those other cities all paired pricing with major transit expansions, which we cannot afford. Pricing alone, without better transit options, will simply punish drivers who have no alternative.',
    stem: 'The point at issue between Alvarez and Brooks is whether',
    choices: [
      { text: 'congestion pricing reduced traffic in the cities that have tried it.' },
      { text: 'the city can afford a major transit expansion.' },
      { text: 'pricing without better transit options would harm drivers who lack alternatives.' },
      { text: 'the city should adopt congestion pricing.' },
      { text: 'cleaner air and faster buses are desirable goals.' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 3,
    explanationQuick:
      'Alvarez explicitly recommends adopting congestion pricing; Brooks\u2019s reply — that pricing alone will "simply punish drivers who have no alternative" — is a case against adopting it. They split yes/no on the policy, while agreeing (or staying silent) on the distractors.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'This one is tricky because the speakers seem to argue past each other, so test each candidate strictly. The first choice looks disputed but isn\u2019t: Alvarez says pricing cut traffic elsewhere, and Brooks\u2019s reply ("those other cities all paired pricing with transit expansions") accepts that traffic fell — Brooks disputes the cause, not the outcome. So both speakers are consistent with the claim that traffic fell; there is no yes/no split. Affordability of transit expansion is Brooks\u2019s claim alone. Harm to drivers without alternatives is Brooks\u2019s claim alone. Desirability of cleaner air is disputed by neither. The genuine split is the policy question: Alvarez says "we should adopt it here" (yes), while Brooks argues that adopting it without transit investment would merely punish captive drivers — a clear case against adoption (no). Implied positions count: Brooks never utters "should not," but a case that adoption would only punish drivers is unmistakably opposition.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect and the subtlest trap (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Brooks never denies that traffic fell in those cities — "those other cities all paired pricing with transit expansions" concedes the outcome while disputing its cause. Since both speakers are consistent with the claim, it is not at issue.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Brooks addresses affordability ("which we cannot afford"); Alvarez never discusses the transit budget or what the city can fund. A point at issue requires a yes from one speaker and a no from the other, and one-sided claims like this one cannot supply both sides.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). Only Brooks makes the harm claim; Alvarez never addresses what pricing-without-transit would do to drivers. There is no opposing stand from Alvarez.',
      'Correct. Alvarez explicitly recommends adoption ("we should adopt it here"), and Brooks\u2019s argument that pricing alone would "simply punish drivers who have no alternative" is a direct case against adopting it. Yes from one, no from the other.',
      'Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Neither speaker questions whether cleaner air and faster buses are desirable; they disagree about the means, not the ends. Undisputed goals cannot be the point at issue.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'A speaker can concede an outcome while disputing its cause — "traffic fell, but because of transit, not pricing" is agreement on the fact and disagreement on the explanation, so check exactly which proposition each speaker affirms or denies.',
