      'Correct. The ban-gets-credit conclusion requires that no rival cause produced the decline. A concurrent cleanup program would be exactly such a rival; learning whether one existed directly tests the argument\u2019s central assumption, with either answer moving our confidence in the causal claim.',
      'Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). Baseline data sounds relevant, but the argument already reports a percentage decline, which is interpretable without the starting share — and no baseline figure could reveal whether the ban or something else caused the drop.',
      'Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The number of neighboring towns has no logical connection to whether Riverton\u2019s ban caused its own litter decline. It is pure scene-setting trivia.',
      'Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). Paper-bag prices concern shoppers\u2019 costs, a different subject from the causal link between the plastic-bag ban and river litter. It shifts the scope from causation to consumer economics.',
