    correctIndex: 0,
    explanationQuick:
      'The principle requires return within 30 days when there is no more-than-normal damage. Rosa\u2019s case meets the condition (normal wear only) and the requirement (returned in three weeks). Every other case either violates the 30-day rule, withholds without qualifying damage, or isn\u2019t a landlord-tenant deposit at all.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Translate the principle into a conditional: if no damage beyond normal wear, then the landlord must return the deposit within 30 days. (The "unless" clause means damage beyond normal wear lifts the duty; it does not forbid returning the deposit anyway.) Now test each scenario. Rosa: condition met (normal wear only), duty satisfied (three weeks is within 30 days) — conforms. Tom: condition met but the deposit was kept — violates the duty. Lee: condition met but returned after 45 days — violates the timing requirement. The homeowner case involves no landlord, no tenant, and no deposit — the principle simply doesn\u2019t apply. Nadia: condition met, returned late — violates, and the vacation excuse appears nowhere in the principle, so it cannot excuse the delay. Only Rosa\u2019s case satisfies both the condition and the requirement.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Correct. The "no damage beyond normal wear" condition is satisfied, triggering the duty to return within 30 days, and the landlord returned it after three weeks — inside the deadline. Both the condition and the requirement of the principle are met.',
      'Incorrect (trap: opposite). With only normal wear, the principle requires return of the deposit; keeping it to cover repainting violates that duty. This scenario does the opposite of what the principle demands.',
      'Incorrect (trap: half-right). It gets the return part right but misses the deadline: 45 days exceeds the 30-day requirement. Partial conformity is still nonconformity when the principle states a deadline.',
      'Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). This involves a homeowner and her sister, not a landlord, tenant, or security deposit. The principle\u2019s conditions are never triggered, so it can neither conform nor violate — it is simply outside the principle\u2019s scope.',
      'Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). The vacation is true but legally irrelevant: the principle states no vacation exception to the 30-day rule. A late return is a late return, whatever the excuse.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'Apply principles mechanically: first check whether the triggering condition is met, then check whether the required action was performed — excuses and partial matches not stated in the principle do not count.',
