      'The principle has the form "may do X only if Y" — Y (all deliverables done by Thursday evening) is necessary for X (working from home Friday). It does not say Y is sufficient to require anything, and "may" grants permission without obligation. Test each case against that logic. Mei: Y met, X done — permitted, no violation. Jonah: X done without Y — the necessary condition fails while the action is taken, which is precisely what "only if" forbids. Priya: Y met, X not done — "may" permits but never requires Friday remote work, so no violation. Sam: Friday remote work with Y met is fine; the principle says nothing about Thursdays, so Thursday remote work is unregulated by it. Alex: with no deliverables due, "all deliverables completed" is vacuously satisfied, so the condition holds. Only Jonah\u2019s case breaks the necessary condition.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect (trap: supported-nonresponsive). Mei satisfies the necessary condition and takes the permitted action — this is exactly what the principle allows. It describes compliance, not violation.',
      'Correct. The principle requires Thursday-evening completion as a necessary condition for Friday remote work, and Jonah took the remote-work action while the condition was unmet. Doing X without the required "only if" condition is the definition of violating this principle.',
      'Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It looks odd — why finish early and still come in? — but "may work from home" grants permission, not an obligation. Declining a permission never violates the rule that grants it.',
      'Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). The principle regulates only Friday remote work; it is silent about Thursdays. Sam\u2019s Friday work satisfies the condition, and his Thursday work falls outside the principle\u2019s scope entirely.',
      'Incorrect (trap: keyword-match). "No deliverables due" might look like a failure to complete things, but completing all of an empty set is vacuously true — the condition is satisfied, so Friday remote work is permitted.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      '"Only if" sets a necessary condition, and "may" grants a permission — violating the principle means taking the action without the condition, not declining the permission or acting where the principle is silent.',
    misconceptionTags: ['necessary-vs-sufficient', 'negation'],
    trapTypes: ['supported-nonresponsive', 'half-right', 'scope-shift', 'keyword-match'],
    prerequisites: ['f-conditional', 'f-translate'],
