    explanationQuick:
      'The original moves from "all successful marathoners follow strict plans" to "Jamal follows a strict plan, so he\u2019ll succeed" — treating a necessary trait as sufficient. The watch argument has the identical form: all genuine Rolexes have engraved serials, this watch has one, so it\u2019s genuine.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'First, name the flaw precisely. "Every successful marathoner follows a strict training plan" makes the plan a necessary condition of success (success → plan). The argument then observes the condition is met (Jamal → plan) and concludes success follows — it treats the necessary condition as sufficient. In conditional terms, it affirms the consequent: from A → B and B, it infers A. The correct parallel must commit the same fallacy with the same structure, not merely share the topic. The watch argument does: genuine → engraved serial; this watch has an engraved serial; therefore genuine. Same necessary-condition-treated-as-sufficient, same affirming-the-consequent form. The distractors differ: the pilot case states the same premises but explicitly refuses the fallacious inference (valid caution); the airline case is a valid universal instantiation (no A are B; x is A; so x is not B); the author case weakens to "most" and "probably," a different flaw of overgeneralization; the rest-days case is valid modus ponens (plan → rest days; Jamal has a plan; so rest days).',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Correct. It replicates the flaw exactly: a necessary trait of the genuine article (engraved serial number) is treated as sufficient to establish genuineness, just as following a strict plan is treated as sufficient for marathon success. Same affirming-the-consequent structure, same necessary/sufficient confusion.',
      'Incorrect and tempting (trap: half-right). It uses parallel premises about pilots and medical exams but explicitly declines to draw the fallacious conclusion ("that alone does not make her a licensed pilot"). Matching the setup while avoiding the flaw is the opposite of parallel flawed reasoning.',
      'Incorrect (trap: answers-different-question). This is a valid argument: from "no budget airline offers first class" and "SwiftAir is a budget airline," the conclusion follows deductively. Parallel-flaw questions require the same flaw, and a valid argument has no flaw to share.',
      'Incorrect (trap: half-right). It resembles the original in topic and in leaping from a trait to success, but its flaw is different: it generalizes from "most" with a merely probabilistic conclusion rather than confusing necessary and sufficient conditions in a deductive form.',
      'Incorrect (trap: opposite). This is valid modus ponens — all strict plans require rest days, Jamal follows one, so he takes rest days. The conclusion genuinely follows, so the reasoning exhibits no flaw at all, let alone the original\u2019s.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'To match a parallel flaw, abstract the original to its logical skeleton (here: A → B; B; therefore A) and require the answer to instantiate that same skeleton — topical similarity without structural identity is a trap.',
