      'A resolve-paradox answer must make both surprising facts compatible, not just restate one of them. The tension here: more police should mean less crime, yet the numbers went up. The key word is "reported" — the statistic measures crimes that get reported, not crimes that occur. The correct choice exploits that gap: a bigger, more visible force can make residents more willing to report offenses they would previously have suffered in silence. If reporting rises faster than actual crime falls, the reported rate climbs while real public safety improves — both facts explained simultaneously. The distractors fail this two-sided test. A doubled population doesn\u2019t explain a rising rate (rates already adjust for population). Desk-duty assignments might explain why crime didn\u2019t fall, but not why it rose. Feeling safer and neighboring towns\u2019 trends leave the core tension untouched.',
