    stimulus:
      'Opponents of bail reform warn that releasing defendants before trial endangers the public. They point to a handful of sensational cases. But those cases are statistical outliers: in jurisdictions that ended cash bail, re-arrest rates for violent offenses did not rise. What did rise was the rate at which defendants appeared for trial — because courts replaced money bail with text-message reminders and transportation assistance. The real lesson is not that reform is reckless; it is that the old system jailed poor people who posed no danger simply because they could not pay. Ending cash bail, paired with court-date supports, protects both liberty and public safety.',
