    stimulus:
      "For decades the city of Harborview has operated an overnight ferry to the mainland, a route that loses money every winter. Admittedly, a consulting firm recently reported that most overnight passengers would simply take a late-night bus if the ferry were discontinued. But the ferry is the only carrier that moves refrigerated freight after 10 p.m., and mainland hospitals rely on those overnight deliveries; thus the ferry's real value is in its cargo service, not its passenger service. Judged by what it actually contributes, then, the overnight ferry deserves continued public funding.",
    stem: "The claim that the ferry's real value is in its cargo service, not its passenger service, plays which one of the following roles in the argument?",
    choices: [
      { text: "It is the argument's main conclusion, which the consulting firm's report is offered to support." },
      { text: "It is an intermediate conclusion, inferred from the facts about refrigerated freight and hospital deliveries, supporting the funding claim in turn." },
      { text: 'It is a premise that directly supports the funding claim even though no other statement in the argument supports it, standing entirely on its own.' },
      { text: 'It is background information describing how the ferry has traditionally been used by island residents.' },
      { text: 'It is the concession the author makes to critics of the ferry before rebutting them directly.' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 1,
    explanationQuick:
      'The cargo-service claim is introduced by "thus," so it is inferred from the freight and hospital evidence — that makes it a conclusion, not a premise or background. But it is not the main conclusion: the final sentence builds on it ("judged by what it actually contributes") to deliver the funding verdict. A claim that is both supported by other claims and used to support a further claim is an intermediate conclusion.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Map the argument layer by layer. The opening sentence about decades of operation and winter losses is scene-setting background. The consulting-firm sentence is a concession: introduced with "admittedly," it acknowledges a point against the ferry — that its passengers could take the bus — without the author endorsing the ferry on those grounds. Then comes the pivot: the "but" introduces the freight facts, and "thus" marks an inference drawn from them, namely that the ferry\'s real value lies in cargo, not passengers. Because it is inferred from other claims, it cannot be a mere premise or background. Finally, the last sentence — "judged by what it actually contributes, then, the overnight ferry deserves continued public funding" — explicitly uses that cargo-value claim as its basis ("what it actually contributes" refers back to it) to reach the funding verdict. So the cargo claim sits in the middle of the structure: supported from below by the freight evidence, supporting from above the funding conclusion. That two-sided position is exactly what defines an intermediate conclusion, and it is what the tempting near-miss choices obscure by collapsing the structure into a single layer.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect (trap: wrong-conclusion). The main conclusion is the funding verdict in the final sentence — the normative "deserves continued public funding" claim everything else points toward. The consulting-firm report actually cuts against the author\'s case, so it cannot be offered in support of anything here.',
      'Correct. The word "thus" shows the cargo-service claim is inferred from the refrigerated-freight and hospital-delivery facts, and the final sentence then leans on that very claim ("judged by what it actually contributes") to conclude the ferry deserves funding. Supported from below and supporting above, it is an intermediate conclusion.',
      'Incorrect (trap: half-right). It is true that the claim supports the funding verdict, but it is false that nothing supports it: "thus" marks it as inferred from the freight and hospital evidence. A premise that is itself argued for is an intermediate conclusion, not a basic premise.',
      'Incorrect (trap: irrelevant-true). Background information sets the scene without being inferred from other claims, but this claim is explicitly derived — "thus" — from the evidence about freight and hospitals. An inferred claim is doing structural work, not scene-setting.',
      'Incorrect (trap: opposite). The concession is the consulting-firm sentence about the late-night bus, which acknowledges a weakness in the ferry\'s case. The cargo-service claim is the rebuttal pivot that answers that concession — the opposite role, turning the argument back toward funding.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'When "thus" or "so" appears mid-argument and the final sentence builds on that claim, the mid-argument claim is an intermediate conclusion — supported from below, supporting above. Keep the concession (the acknowledged weakness) and the pivot (the answer to it) in separate boxes.',
    misconceptionTags: ['conclusion-vs-premise'],
    trapTypes: ['wrong-conclusion', 'half-right', 'irrelevant-true', 'opposite'],
