    stimulus:
      'Airlines routinely overbook flights, yet few would call the practice dishonest. The justification is straightforward: a predictable share of ticket holders will not show up, and spreading the cost of those empty seats across all passengers keeps fares lower for everyone who does fly. The same reasoning applies to restaurants that charge diners who reserve tables and then fail to appear. A predictable share of reservations go unfulfilled, and a modest no-show fee spreads that cost so that menu prices stay lower for the diners who do show up. The city\'s proposed ban on restaurant no-show fees should therefore be rejected: it would force responsible diners to subsidize the diners who waste tables.',
    stem: 'Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning used in the argument?',
    choices: [
      { text: 'It shows that a principle underlying the airline practice leads to an unacceptable consequence when applied to restaurants, and it rejects the restaurant practice on that basis.' },
      { text: "It cites the airlines' overbooking records as statistical evidence proving that no-show fees lower menu prices." },
      { text: 'It argues that because the airline and restaurant cases are alike in every relevant respect, the restaurant practice must be acceptable.' },
      { text: 'It uses the airline case to articulate a general principle about spreading the costs of predictable no-shows, applies it to restaurant reservations, and concludes the ban should be rejected.' },
      { text: 'It demonstrates that supporters of the ban overlooked the effect of no-shows on menu prices, and it rejects the ban solely because of that oversight.' },
    ],
    correctIndex: 3,
    explanationQuick:
      'The airline example is not offered as data, and the argument never claims the two cases match in every detail. Instead the airline case is used to state a general principle — spreading the cost of predictable no-shows keeps prices lower for everyone — which is then applied to restaurants to oppose the ban. That principle-mediated move is exactly what the correct choice describes.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Break the reasoning into three steps. First, the airline case is introduced not as evidence but as an illustration: from it the author extracts a general principle, namely that when no-shows are predictable, spreading their cost across all users keeps prices lower for everyone who shows up. The key phrase is "the same reasoning applies" — the reasoning, the principle, is what carries over, not a point-by-point similarity between airlines and restaurants. Second, that principle is applied to the new case: restaurant no-show fees do the same cost-spreading work for menu prices. Third, the principle-backed defense of the practice yields the verdict that the proposed ban should be rejected. The near-miss choice about the cases being "alike in every relevant respect" overstates the argument: the author never claims exhaustive similarity and does not need it, since the principle does the justificatory work. The other distractors misread the analogy entirely — as statistical proof, as a reductio against the restaurant practice, or as an exposé of the ban\'s supporters — none of which the stimulus attempts.',
    choiceExplanations: [
      'Incorrect (trap: opposite). This describes a reductio argument that rejects the restaurant practice, but the argument does the reverse: it endorses the restaurant practice by means of the airline case and rejects the ban. The direction of the reasoning is inverted.',
      'Incorrect (trap: scope-shift). No overbooking records or statistics are ever cited; "a predictable share" is a general observation used to state a principle, not a dataset. This choice recasts an illustrative analogy as empirical proof, shifting what the airline case is doing.',
      'Incorrect (trap: too-strong). This is the tempting near-miss: the argument does move from airlines to restaurants, but it never claims the cases are alike in every relevant respect. The work is done by the stated general principle ("the same reasoning applies"), which needs no exhaustive similarity.',
      'Correct. The airline case articulates the principle — predictable no-show costs, spread across users, keep prices lower for all — and that principle is then applied to restaurant reservations to defend no-show fees and reject the ban. This captures the principle-mediated structure exactly.',
      'Incorrect (trap: unsupported). The argument never discusses what the ban\'s supporters believe, overlooked, or intended, so no claim about their oversight can be part of its method. The rejection of the ban rests on the cost-spreading principle, not on anyone\'s inattention.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
      'When an argument says "the same reasoning applies," look for the general principle doing the work — the method is principle-then-application, not a claim that the two cases match in every detail. "In every relevant respect" is almost always too strong.',
    misconceptionTags: ['scope-shift', 'strength-mismatch'],
    trapTypes: ['opposite', 'scope-shift', 'too-strong', 'unsupported'],
    prerequisites: ['f-structure'],
    estimatedSeconds: 95,
    sourceType: 'original',
    hints: [
      'What job is the airline example doing — is it presented as data to prove something, or as a way of stating a general idea?',
      'Find the general principle the argument states outright (something about spreading costs and lower prices). Where does the argument go on to apply it?',
      'Compare "the same reasoning applies" with "the cases are alike in every relevant respect." Does the argument ever claim the second, stronger thing, or does the principle do all the work?',
    ],
