    explanationWalkthrough:
      'Translate "every" as "all" and line up the chain: most referred patients are diagnosed; all diagnosed patients get treatment plans; all treatment plans are reviewed quarterly. The chain begins with "most," and "most" chained through any number of "all" statements stays "most" — the weakest link caps the conclusion. So the licensed inference is that most referred patients have plans that are reviewed each quarter. Now audit each choice\'s quantifier against that cap. The first three choices each take a claim the chain supports at "most" strength and upgrade it to "every": most referred are diagnosed becomes every referred is diagnosed; most referred get plans becomes every referred gets a plan; most referred have reviewed plans becomes every referred has a reviewed plan. The fourth choice is subtler: the chain does guarantee that some reviewed plans belong to referred patients, but it reverses direction and upgrades "some" to "most" — the reviewed plans could largely belong to patients diagnosed without referral. Only the final choice keeps every quantifier exactly where the chain puts it.',
