    itemPurpose: 'mixed-discrimination',
    validationStatus: 'validated',
    stimulus:
      'Priya: "Converting Elm Street into a pedestrian mall will revitalize downtown. The mall will draw more foot traffic, and foot traffic is what the area\'s struggling storefronts need most."\n\nMarcus: "Even if the mall draws more foot traffic, that increase will not rescue the storefronts that are failing because of high rents rather than low visibility. The mall\'s defenders are asking the wrong question: what matters is not whether foot traffic rises, but whether the mall does anything about the operating costs that are actually driving these shops under."',
    stem: 'The point at issue between Priya and Marcus is whether',
    choices: [
      { text: "an increase in foot traffic would help downtown's struggling storefronts" },
      { text: 'converting Elm Street into a pedestrian mall would increase foot traffic downtown' },
      { text: 'high rents are contributing to the struggles of some downtown storefronts' },
      { text: 'the city should convert Elm Street into a pedestrian mall' },
      { text: "reducing operating costs would help downtown's struggling storefronts" },
    ],
    correctIndex: 0,
    explanationQuick:
      'Marcus\'s "even if" concedes the foot-traffic increase rather than disputing it, so the two only appear to disagree there. But Priya claims foot traffic is what struggling storefronts need most, while Marcus claims it will not rescue them because high rents are the real problem — they genuinely take opposite sides on whether more foot traffic would help.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
      'A point at issue requires both speakers to address the same proposition and take incompatible stands on it, so test each choice against both speakers. The tempting choice about whether the mall would increase foot traffic fails that test: Marcus\'s "even if" grants the increase for the sake of argument instead of denying it, so there is no genuine disagreement — they merely seem to engage on that point. The real clash is one step downstream. Priya asserts that foot traffic is what the struggling storefronts need most, which commits her to the view that more foot traffic would help them. Marcus directly denies this: the increase "will not rescue" the failing storefronts, because their problem is high rents rather than low visibility. Those are incompatible positions on the same proposition, which makes it the point at issue. The remaining choices each fail the two-speaker test: only Marcus addresses high rents, only Marcus implies anything about the mall itself (he criticizes its defenders\' reasoning without taking a stand on the proposal), and only Marcus implies that lower operating costs would matter.',
